{"timestamp":"2026-10-18T04:29:56.551Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"PLAN","details":{"toolName":"task","args":{"description":"Build SystemOne decision probe","prompt":"You are building the measurement probe for a \"Tier-1 typed-decision endpoint\" evaluation in a NeoForge mod repo's Python sidecar (repo: /Users/bbaaxx/.kimi-code-adjacent path: /Users/bbaaxx/Code/projects/siasworkshop). The sidecar lives in `sidecar/` (stdlib-only Python, plain-script tests run as `python3 <file>.py` exiting non-zero on failure, no pytest, no pip deps in the sidecar itself). A separate setup (running in parallel, not your concern) downloads and serves two local decision models behind a TypeSafe \"SystemOne\"-shaped HTTP API: `POST {endpoint}/v1/systemone` with body `{\"state\": <str>, \"questions\": {<name>: {\"type\": \"choice\", \"instructions\": <str>, \"criteria\": {<option_key>: <description>}} | {<name>: {\"type\": \"noul\", \"instructions\": <str>}}}}`. Per the SDK docs, a choice answer carries a probability per option + the most likely option + a confidence; a noul answer carries P(yes); the response also has a usage block. THE EXACT WIRE KEY NAMES ARE NOT YET VERIFIED — keep all response parsing in ONE small tolerant function so a live-server adjustment is a one-line change.\n\nYou own exactly three files: `sidecar/probe_decision.py` (new), `sidecar/corpus_score.py` (extend, existing tests must stay green), `sidecar/test_decision_probe.py` (new). Do NOT touch: session.py, run_corpus.py, mockmod.py, model.py, catalog.py, sidecar.py, tap_import.py, any corpus/*.json, README.md, mdocs/, metrics/, Java.\n\nRead first: `sidecar/corpus_score.py` (classify/score_run/jeva_metrics/summarize + the row schema docstring), `sidecar/run_corpus.py` (CLI conventions, the seed scheme `seed = scenario.seed + trial*1000 + perm`, the 19-key corpus JSONL row schema incl. board_order), `sidecar/corpus/unroutable_anchor_first_listed.json` and `sidecar/corpus/null_board_cooling.json` (scenario + assert schema), `sidecar/test_corpus.py` (test conventions, [PASS]/[FAIL] prints, exit non-zero).\n\n=== DELIVERABLE 1: `sidecar/probe_decision.py` ===\n\nPurpose: drive the corpus scenarios against ANY SystemOne-compatible endpoint (a typed decision model — e.g. Kev-4B, Jev-Style-0.8B) and record corpus JSONL rows for scoring. STDIO-free, stdlib only (urllib, json, argparse, random, time, pathlib).\n\nRendering (per run):\n- Load a corpus scenario JSON (same schema as run_corpus: name/player_name/seed/trials/permutations/observation/open_tasks[{key,type,detail,claimable}]/assert{forbid_claim_keys,null_ground_truth}).\n- Seeding: EXACTLY run_corpus's scheme — `seed = scenario.get(\"seed\",0) + trial*1000 + perm`, `rng = random.Random(seed)`; when permutations>1, shuffle a deep copy of open_tasks with rng. `board_order` = the permuted keys (list) — this is also the ORDER OPTIONS ARE SENT.\n- state = `scenario[\"observation\"] + \"\\n\\nOPEN TASKS:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(\"- \" + key + \" — \" + detail for each task)` (em dash U+2014, same visual shape the LLM paths use).\n- ONE POST per run with TWO questions (question isolation is a native API property — the coverage/null question must NEVER be an option, JEVA Prop 2):\n  - `claim`: {\"type\":\"choice\",\"instructions\":\"Which listed open task should this agent claim right now?\",\"criteria\":{key: detail for each task IN THE PERMUTED ORDER}}\n  - `coverage`: {\"type\":\"noul\",\"instructions\":\"Is any listed open task claimable by this agent right now?\"}\n  No position identifiers anywhere (no numbers/bullets in criteria).\n- Derive the decision (client side, recorded): `coverage_no = P(yes) < 0.5`; decision_class = \"rest\" if coverage_no else \"claim\"; chosen_key = argmax-probability option of the claim answer (independent of the coverage answer).\n\nResponse parsing — ONE function `parse_systemone(body: dict, question_names) -> dict[name -> {probs: {opt: float}, answer: str, p_yes: float|None}]`, tolerant of key spelling variants (answers/results; probabilities as dict or list-of-{option,probability}; choice/answer/noul; noul as bool or {\"yes\": p} or p_yes field). On total mismatch raise a ValueError whose message includes the body's top-level keys (so a live-wire fix is trivial). P(yes): for noul, the probability of \"yes\" however spelled.\n\nRow (extends the corpus 19-key schema; one JSON object per line appended to --out):\n{\"scenario\",\"trial\",\"permutation\",\"seed\",\"mode\",\"model\",\"tier0\":false,\"decision_class\",\"chosen_key\",\"correct\",\"failure_class_hit\",\"invalid_reason\",\"coverage_any_claimable\" (bool from P(yes)>=0.5),\"coverage_confidence\" (float P(yes) — the soft score),\"completion_tokens\" (None if usage absent),\"prompt_tokens\" (from usage or None),\"latency_ms\" (client-measured),\"reward\" (Player-1 mapped: correct=+1 else 0; invalid/transport/parse failure = -1 + invalid_reason set),\"board_order\",\"option_probs\" (full dict),\"p_yes\": same as coverage_confidence}\nCorrectness: reuse `corpus_score.classify`-compatible logic WITHOUT inventing a new scorer — call corpus_score.score_run with a classified dict you build from the derived decision (tool=\"claim\"/\"rest\", exec_outcome=\"exec_ok\" — a typed endpoint's decision is a decision, execution is the mod's business downstream; null-board rest when null_ground_truth → correct; claiming a forbidden key → failure_class_hit). Read corpus_score.py first and fit its existing signatures.\n\nCLI: `--corpus PATH` (file or dir of *.json), `--endpoint URL` (default http://127.0.0.1:8010), `--tag NAME` (default \"typed\"; recorded as mode `systemone:<tag>` and in --out filename), `--trials N`, `--permutations K` (overrides; defaults from scenario), `--out PATH` (default `metrics/corpus-<tag>-<ts>.jsonl` cwd-relative append), `--quiet`. Exit 0 (measurement tool). Structure the core as `run(args, http_post=None)` where http_post is injectable for tests (callable(url, payload_dict, timeout) -> dict); default implementation uses urllib with a 120s timeout, raising a clean error on non-2xx with the response body preview.\n\n=== DELIVERABLE 2: `corpus_score.py` extension ===\n\nAdd PURE functions (no I/O), stdlib only, do not modify existing function behavior:\n- `null_auc(rows, fpr_target=0.05)` — rows carry `coverage_confidence` (float P(yes), skip rows where None) and share one `null_ground_truth` (pass it in, or take rows that each carry it in \"null_ground_truth\"). Detection task: POSITIVE = null board (nothing claimable). A good model says P(yes)≈0 on null boards, so detection_score = 1 - coverage_confidence. Return {\"auc\": float|None, \"tpr_at_fpr\": float|None, \"fpr_target\": fpr_target, \"n\": n} computed from the ROC (rank-based Mann-Whitney AUC is fine; TPR at the largest threshold achieving FPR <= target). auc None when <1 positive or <1 negative row.\n- Integrate: when rows carry float coverage_confidence, `jeva_metrics` should ALSO include this null_auc block (keep the existing confusion matrix for binary answers).\n=== DELIVERABLE 3: `sidecar/test_decision_probe.py` ===\n\nPlain-script test (exit non-zero on failure; [PASS]/[FAIL] prints). In-thread stub HTTP server (http.server on an ephemeral port, run in a daemon thread) speaking `/v1/systemone` from a SCRIPTED per-request table: the stub inspects the payload's criteria order and returns answers from a queue/table keyed on that order. Define the stub's response body using the DOCUMENTED SDK shape: {\"answers\": {\"claim\": {\"probabilities\": {key: p...}, \"choice\": <key>, \"confidence\": 0-1}, \"coverage\": {\"p_yes\": <float>}}, \"usage\": {\"prompt_tokens\": n, \"completion_tokens\": n}}. Cases:\n1. End-to-end: two scenarios (the unroutable-anchor file + the null-board file from corpus/) at trials=2 permutations=2 via the stub with a scripted GOOD policy (always picks the permitted key, P(yes)=0.9 when claimable / 0.1 on null board) → all rows correct, reward +1, board_order varies with permutation, coverage_any_claimable False on the null board.\n2. Option-order caveat: scripted policy keyed to ORDER (claims the FIRST option in whatever order it receives; P(yes) always 0.9) → chosen_key flips across permutations (flip_rate 1.0 via corpus_score.jeva_metrics), failure_class_hit on unroutable trials.\n3. null_auc: mixed synthetic rows (perfect separation, reversed, single-class → None).\n4. Wire mismatch: stub returns {\"unexpected\": true} → the parse error names the top-level keys (assert the error message mentions them).\n5. Transport failure: http_post raising ConnectionError → row recorded with invalid_reason and reward -1 (run continues, exit still 0; assert the row).\nUse tmp --out paths; never write into the repo's metrics/.\n\nVerification before reporting (from `sidecar/`): `python3 test_decision_probe.py && python3 test_corpus.py && python3 test_tap_import.py` — all must pass (test_corpus.py must still be 56 checks green; do not weaken existing tests). Report: files changed, exact verification output summary, any deviations from this spec and why."}}}
